NumPy functions that create, validate, summarize, categorize, and scan those readings.
A valid recorded temperature is from 12.0 through 30.0, inclusive.
2. Operations
Operation 1: Create a temperature array
def create_water_temperature_array(values: list) -> np.ndarray:
• Convert values to a one-dimensional NumPy array.
• The returned dtype must be np.float64.
• Preserve input order.
Operation 2: Validate a temperature array
def validate_water_temperature_array(arr: np.ndarray) -> bool:
• Return Python boolean True only when arr is non-empty.
• Its dtype is a numeric NumPy subtype.
• Every value is from 12.0 to 30.0, inclusive.
• Return False for an empty, non-numeric, or out-of-range array.
• A non-numeric array must not cause an exception.
Operation 3: Compute temperature statistics
def compute_water_temperature_stats(arr: np.ndarray) -> tuple:
• Return (mean, standard_deviation, maximum, minimum).
• Use np.mean and np.std with default ddof=0.
• Round all four values to two decimal places and return Python float values.
Operation 4: Categorize temperatures
def categorize_water_temperatures(arr: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:
Category Temperature Range
Cold 12.0 <= value <= 18.0
Optimal 18.0 < value <= 26.0
Warm 26.0 < value <= 30.0
Invalid value below 12.0 or above 30.0
Return a NumPy string array with one category per input value.
Boundary example: [12, 18, 18.1, 26, 26.1, 30, 30.1] → [Cold, Cold, Optimal, Optimal, Warm, Warm, Invalid].
Operation 5: Find the longest warm streak
def longest_warm_streak(arr: np.ndarray) -> int:
